WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.100
SPEAKER: Optimizing
GKE clusters for cost

00:00:02.100 --> 00:00:04.380
is not a one-time exercise.

00:00:04.380 --> 00:00:07.740
It's a longer journey to shift
team and organizational culture

00:00:07.740 --> 00:00:11.500
while incrementally
implementing best practices.

00:00:11.500 --> 00:00:14.220
One of these best practices
is to use monitoring systems

00:00:14.220 --> 00:00:17.100
specific to GKE to
help establish cost

00:00:17.100 --> 00:00:19.560
optimization as a discipline.

00:00:19.560 --> 00:00:21.540
Let's take a look at
why we should do this

00:00:21.540 --> 00:00:23.536
and how it can be done.

00:00:23.536 --> 00:00:26.404
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:30.710 --> 00:00:33.320
As you may have seen in
previous videos from the series,

00:00:33.320 --> 00:00:35.540
there are a handful
of critical areas

00:00:35.540 --> 00:00:40.450
to practice cost optimization
in a GKE environment.

00:00:40.450 --> 00:00:42.250
We'll focus on
three of these areas

00:00:42.250 --> 00:00:44.680
and how they fit into a
monitoring system for cost

00:00:44.680 --> 00:00:49.080
optimization, specifically
culture, bin packing, and app

00:00:49.080 --> 00:00:50.600
right-sizing.

00:00:50.600 --> 00:00:53.060
Let's start by
discussing culture.

00:00:53.060 --> 00:00:55.820
Teams who own GKE
environments are not simply

00:00:55.820 --> 00:00:59.460
trying to drive down costs
while ignoring everything else.

00:00:59.460 --> 00:01:02.390
They instead must balance
cost-saving techniques

00:01:02.390 --> 00:01:05.090
while ensuring the
applications running on GKE

00:01:05.090 --> 00:01:08.660
are still performant, stable,
and continue to deliver value

00:01:08.660 --> 00:01:10.450
to their users.

00:01:10.450 --> 00:01:12.250
To effectively
balance all of this,

00:01:12.250 --> 00:01:15.070
teams have begun incorporating
cloud financial management,

00:01:15.070 --> 00:01:18.060
or FinOps, into their culture.

00:01:18.060 --> 00:01:20.310
This is the practice of
building systems for financial

00:01:20.310 --> 00:01:24.440
accountability in the
pay-as-you-go cloud world.

00:01:24.440 --> 00:01:27.200
Now, put this into the
context of Kubernetes.

00:01:27.200 --> 00:01:29.630
Rapid adoption of
Kubernetes in particular

00:01:29.630 --> 00:01:32.450
means that many teams are
still learning and establishing

00:01:32.450 --> 00:01:34.700
their own best practices.

00:01:34.700 --> 00:01:36.392
This is true when
it comes to managing

00:01:36.392 --> 00:01:37.850
the cost of running
their clusters,

00:01:37.850 --> 00:01:41.060
especially as they grow.

00:01:41.060 --> 00:01:42.950
Continuous monitoring
of these environments

00:01:42.950 --> 00:01:45.800
enables the necessary
cultural shift.

00:01:45.800 --> 00:01:49.010
When teams have proper tuning
and data at their disposal

00:01:49.010 --> 00:01:51.470
to practice cost
optimization, the awareness

00:01:51.470 --> 00:01:54.140
shifts the discipline earlier
in the software development

00:01:54.140 --> 00:01:55.930
lifecycle.

00:01:55.930 --> 00:01:57.480
So what specifically
should we aim

00:01:57.480 --> 00:02:00.610
to accomplish with
monitoring here?

00:02:00.610 --> 00:02:02.770
First, we'll want to
focus on a cluster

00:02:02.770 --> 00:02:05.470
administrator-centric
monitoring system.

00:02:05.470 --> 00:02:09.150
We can do so with a focus
on cluster bin packing.

00:02:09.150 --> 00:02:12.270
Bin packing is the ability to
effectively schedule workloads

00:02:12.270 --> 00:02:15.930
across nodes in a cluster, in
such a way that ensures there

00:02:15.930 --> 00:02:17.820
are little wasted resources--

00:02:17.820 --> 00:02:20.510
CPU and memory, for example.

00:02:20.510 --> 00:02:22.220
Efficient bin
packing is achieved

00:02:22.220 --> 00:02:25.610
with a real-time understanding
of how much resources workloads

00:02:25.610 --> 00:02:28.670
request, and how
those workloads fit

00:02:28.670 --> 00:02:31.520
into the various amounts
of allocatable resources

00:02:31.520 --> 00:02:35.010
that the VMs and the
cluster have available.

00:02:35.010 --> 00:02:36.840
Cluster administrators,
when empowered

00:02:36.840 --> 00:02:38.610
with the right
monitoring data, can

00:02:38.610 --> 00:02:41.430
improve how they support
cluster tenants, while also

00:02:41.430 --> 00:02:45.470
ensuring that provision
infrastructure is not wasted.

00:02:45.470 --> 00:02:47.210
We can even set up
alerting policies

00:02:47.210 --> 00:02:49.850
on the data collected for
the most egregious cases

00:02:49.850 --> 00:02:52.250
of overprovisioning to
proactively kick off

00:02:52.250 --> 00:02:55.590
the process of
cost optimization.

00:02:55.590 --> 00:02:59.170
Developers can also benefit
from a different perspective--

00:02:59.170 --> 00:03:01.530
a monitoring system that
captures recommendations

00:03:01.530 --> 00:03:03.950
for app right-sizing.

00:03:03.950 --> 00:03:06.170
This enables developers
to better understand

00:03:06.170 --> 00:03:08.420
how much of the
requested resources

00:03:08.420 --> 00:03:11.800
are actually being utilized
by their application.

00:03:11.800 --> 00:03:13.570
With a deep understanding
of scenarios

00:03:13.570 --> 00:03:15.280
where they may be
overprovisioned

00:03:15.280 --> 00:03:17.410
in their workload
requests, developers

00:03:17.410 --> 00:03:19.240
can make informed
decisions on how

00:03:19.240 --> 00:03:22.110
they can easily reduce cost.

00:03:22.110 --> 00:03:24.330
Now that we've looked at
the importance of culture,

00:03:24.330 --> 00:03:27.000
bin packing, and app
right-sizing for GKE cost

00:03:27.000 --> 00:03:29.550
optimization, the next
step is setting up

00:03:29.550 --> 00:03:31.620
the monitoring system.

00:03:31.620 --> 00:03:34.410
Be sure to check out the paper
linked in the description.

00:03:34.410 --> 00:03:36.300
We publish an
end-to-end guide on how

00:03:36.300 --> 00:03:39.150
you can set up such a monitoring
system with Google Cloud

00:03:39.150 --> 00:03:41.060
Monitoring.

00:03:41.060 --> 00:03:44.060
In the next video, we'll cover
how cluster administrators can

00:03:44.060 --> 00:03:46.430
set up monitoring for
cluster bin packing

00:03:46.430 --> 00:03:48.530
to optimize costs on GKE.

00:03:48.530 --> 00:03:50.030
Stay tuned.

00:03:50.030 --> 00:03:53.380
